{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## AC1 / AC2 / AC3 — proven from a real `terraform plan`, no apply\n\n**Falsifiable claim under test.** `aws_instance.gerrit`'s `user_data` is rejected because the\nRENDERED template is 16,668 bytes, 284 over EC2's 16,384-byte cap; encoding it as\n`user_data_base64 = base64gzip(templatefile(...))` reduces the transported payload to ~6.9 KB\nand lets the plan generate as an IN-PLACE update. Disproof would have been any of: the plan\nstill failing; the plan showing `aws_instance.gerrit` replaced or `to destroy > 0`; or the\nencoded payload not decompressing to the intended script. None occurred.\n\n**RED, before the change** — `terraform plan -lock=false` in a fresh worktree at `origin/main`\n(`84ef284833be`), rc=1, exactly ONE error in the whole run:\n\n```\nError: expected length of user_data to be in the range (0 - 16384), got #!/usr/bin/env bash ...\n  with aws_instance.gerrit, on main.tf line 88\n```\n\nSo the overflow was the sole blocker, not one of several.\n\n**AC1 — the plan GENERATES.** After the change, rc=**2** (drift), not 1 (error). Zero `Error:`\nlines. Terraform reached the point of printing an execution plan for the whole configuration.\n\n**AC2 — no replacement, nothing destroyed.**\n\n```\n  # aws_instance.gerrit will be updated in-place\n  ~ resource \"aws_instance\" \"gerrit\" {\n        id = \"i-00880b2c7f13527c5\"\n      + user_data_base64 = \"H4sIAAAA...\"\n...\nPlan: 2 to add, 25 to change, 0 to destroy.\n```\n\n`grep -c \"forces replacement|must be replaced|will be destroyed\"` over the plan output → **0**.\nThis is read from the plan, not from `user_data_replace_on_change`'s documented default, which\nis what the ticket asked for. (The 25 changes are the pre-existing drift backlog — the 11\nalarms from 09-03 plus tonight's 2683 alarm work — not something this change introduces.)\n\n**AC3 — the payload decodes to exactly the intended script, PARTIALLY established.**\nDecoding the `user_data_base64` value terraform actually planned:\n\n```\nbase64 characters                       9,256\ngzip bytes delivered as UserData        6,941   (limit 16,384; 9,443 B headroom)\ngzip magic                              1f 8b   (what cloud-init sniffs)\ndecompressed                           16,668   (i.e. 284 over the cap uncompressed — the bug)\nfirst line                             #!/usr/bin/env bash\n```\n\nDiffed against the template, the decompressed payload differs ONLY in the three declared\ninterpolations (`data_volume_id` → `vol-06fa2e77a9dd97527`, `gate_scratch_volume_id` →\n`vol-06780b8557d1416b7`, `gate_scratch_mount` → `/var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch`) and the seven\n`$${` → `${` un-escapings — 9 hunks, all accounted for. The script cloud-init would run is\nbyte-identical to the rendered template.\n\n**What I did NOT establish, and cannot without an action outside my scope:** that cloud-init on\nthis AL2023 arm64 AMI accepts the gzip encoding *at boot*. That needs a boot, and `user_data`\nchanges only take effect on next boot — which is also why this is safe to apply now. The\nevidence for it is that the payload carries the gzip magic and round-trips exactly, plus\ncloud-init's long-standing documented support for compressed user-data; the evidence is NOT a\nbooted instance. An operator replacing or rebooting this host should confirm\n`/var/log/cloud-init-output.log` shows `user_data.sh complete.` before trusting it.\n\nNo AWS mutation was performed. Every plan ran with `-lock=false`, so not even the S3 state lock\nwas written."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788647047882917001,"uuid":"e67aa23d-f104-4006-a969-6cef3fb370f8"}